there's a separate sequence? The corrupt fixture is hand-written, could just be illustrative. Risk. Let me hedge: sort by a key that uses leading numeric prefix if present, else mtime+name. Overengineering.\n\nAlternative robust approach: sort by `(st_mtime_ns, name)`. For same-mtime writes that are counter-ordered, name tie-break gives correct order if names prefix. For distinct mtime, correct. The only failure mode: mtimes equal AND names not ordered \u2014 same as name-only failure. And name-only failure mode: names not ordered. So `(mtime, name)` is at least as good as name-only, except if writer sets mtimes in non-creation order (extremely unlikely) or if a file is copied later. Recovery with os.replace preserves mtime. So `(mtime_ns, name)` seems best.\n\nBut there's one more subtlety: 0.01s sleep uses time.sleep; file mtimes set at write. Distinct. Good.\n\nI'll go with `(st_mtime_ns, f.name)`.\n\nHmm, actually consider D4 more: both files created via two send_notice calls; distinct mtime. Order preserved. Good.\n\nWait, but what if the OS coalesces timestamps due to coarse clock? ext4/tmpfs fine.\n\nLet me also consider whether `send_notice` writes files with `.json` extension. The test globs `*.json`, so yes.\n\nNow, a big unknown: `send_notice` signature. Test: `smn.send_notice(direction=..., seat=..., job_id=..., profile_home=str(...), env=self.env)`. So keyword args accepted. Our module doesn't call send_notice, so irrelevant. Good\u2014we only read files.\n\nAlso, we should ensure `prepare_turn` does not require importing seat_move_notice. Standalone. But maybe reuse formatting? No.\n\nNow, the \"in-flight directory\" naming: test uses `_inflight_dir = mailbox / \"inflight\"`. Must match exactly. Good.\n\nNow, D7 deployment record content requirements:\n- contains \"inflight\" (lowercase check on src.lower(), so any case)\n- contains \"consume\"\n- contains \"20261106_173554_a10444\"\n- contains \"state.db\"\n\nI'll write comprehensive markdown.\n\nAlso maybe update README.md to document consume-after-success mechanism and live verification, and mention seat-move-turn.md. The editable set includes README.md, so update it to reflect the new drain flow (prepare_turn/consume_turn) rather than the old drain_notices approach.\n\nAnd seat-move-notice.py existing entry point\u2014does it need changes? The editable set includes it. Maybe add a CLI for the turn launcher? Not required by tests. But maybe the deployment record references a launcher script `seat-move-turn.py`. Should I modify seat-move-notice.py? The task says editable; changing not strictly needed. But perhaps the intent is to add a `--turn` mode? Let me keep it mostly and maybe no change. However, the user included it as editable\u2014maybe expecting us to wire it? The bar doesn't test it. I'll leave it unchanged to avoid risk. Hmm, but \"files list\" includes it, no obligation to change.\n\nActually, maybe D1-D6 errors=6 include import errors. Once module exists, they pass. Good.\n\nWait, one more: the test module name `kit.seat_move_drain`. Need `kit/__init__.py`? Tests import `importlib.import_module(\"kit.seat_move_notice\")` already in job 5, so `kit` is a package. Fine.\n\nNow let's double check D2 with our recovery order logic. After first prepare: mailbox empty, inflight has notice with original mtime. Second prepare: recover moves back (mtime preserved). files sorted \u2192 one. parse \u2192 okay. stage. returns context2 with id once. Then consume_turn removes. assertions: mailbox glob empty, inflight glob empty. Our consume removes files; maybe remove dir too. glob empty anyway. Good.\n\nBut: our prepare creates inflight dir even if mailbox empty? No\u2014return before mkdir when no files. D3 inflight glob empty (dir nonexistent \u2192 glob returns empty). Good.\n\nPotential problem: In D2's first prepare, we create inflight and move. Second prepare recovers then stages. But wait, `_recover_inflight` uses mailbox.mkdir? mailbox already exists. When moving back, mailbox exists. Fine. But what if mailbox didn't exist but inflight did? Then need to mkdir mailbox first. Add `mailbox.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` before replace. But D3: no dirs, skip. We only mkdir if inflight has files.\n\nImplementation of `_recover_inflight`:\n```\ndef _recover_inflight(mailbox, inflight):\n    if not inflight.is_dir():\n        return\n    staged = sorted(inflight.glob(\"*.json\"), key=_order_key)\n    if staged:\n        mailbox.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        for p in staged:\n            os.replace(p, mailbox / p.name)\n    try:\n        inflight.rmdir()\n    except OSError:\n        pass\n```\nrmdir when non-empty raises; ignore.\n\nBut careful: after recovery, inflight dir removed. Then staging recreates. Fine. If inflight had no json but some other file, rmdir fails silently, dir remains; then staging later: we mkdir exist_ok. Fine. But D3 cleanup: if inflight dir preexists empty? Not in tests.\n\nHmm, one issue: D1 asserts `_inflight_dir(...).glob(\"*.json\")` non-empty after staging. Yes.\n\nNow edge: file ordering for recovery. Recovery order doesn't matter.\n\nNow formatting and \"exactly once\": Let me make sure no duplicate. Also maybe include a header that doesn't contain notice ids.\n\nLet me write the module.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Stage seat-move notices for one conductor turn.\n\nThe Plumbline mailbox (``runtime/plumbline-seat-notices``) is written by side\nprocesses through ``kit.seat_move_notice``.  The conductor's turn launcher uses\nthis module to hand those notices to the Bot Chat exactly once:\n\n* :func:`prepare_turn` recovers notices left staged by a previous failed turn,\n  drains the mailbox in FIFO order into the ``inflight`` staging directory, and\n  returns the parsed notices plus the context string to announce.\n* :func:`consume_turn` is called only after the turn completes successfully.\n  Until it is called, the notices remain in ``inflight`` and a later\n  :func:`prepare_turn` recovers and re-announces them.\n\nA crash between staging and consumption therefore loses nothing: the notice is\nstill on disk and is announced on the retry.  A clean turn removes the staged\nfiles, so a notice is never announced twice.\n\nEverything here operates inside the given profile home.  The module never\nwrites a Hermes session, never shells out to ``hermes``, and claims no lease.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nimport pathlib\nfrom typing import Iterable, Mapping\n\n__all__ = [\"prepare_turn\", \"consume_turn\", \"format_context\", \"mailbox_dir\", \"inflight_dir\"]\n\n_MAILBOX_REL = pathlib.Path(\"runtime\") / \"plumbline-seat-notices\"\n_INFLIGHT = \"inflight\"\n_FLAG = \"PLUMBLINE_SEAT_NOTICE\"\n\n\ndef _env_flag(env: Mapping[str,str] | None) -> bool:\n    source = os.environ if env is None else env\n    return str(source.get(_FLAG, \"1\")).strip() != \"0\"\n\n\ndef mailbox_dir(profile_home) -> pathlib.Path:\n    return pathlib.Path(profile_home).expanduser() / _MAILBOX_REL\n\n\ndef inflight_dir(profile_home) -> pathlib.Path:\n    return mailbox_dir(profile_home) / _INFLIGHT\n\n\ndef _order_key(path: pathlib.Path):\n    try:\n        mtime = path.stat().st_mtime_ns\n    except OSError:\n        mtime = 0\n    return (mtime, path.name)\n\n\ndef _load_notice(path):\n    with path.open(\"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n        data = json.load(fh)   # raises JSONDecodeError on corruption\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(f\"seat notice {path.name!r} is not a JSON object\")\n    return data\n\n\ndef _recover_inflight(mailbox, inflight):\n    if not inflight.is_dir():\n        return\n    staged = sorted(inflight.glob(\"*.json\"), key=_order_key)\n    if staged:\n        mailbox.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        for p in staged:\n            os.replace(p, mailbox / p.name)\n    try:\n        inflight.rmdir()\n    except OSError:\n        pass\n\n\ndef format_context(notices):\n    if not notices:\n        return \"\"\n    lines = [\"Seat-move notices awaiting announcement:\"]\n    for n in notices:\n        direction = n.get(\"direction\", \"?\")\n        seat = n.get(\"seat\", \"?\")\n        job = n.get(\"job_id\", \"?\")\n        notice_id = n.get(\"notice_id\", \"?\")\n        lines.append(f\"- {direction}: seat {seat}, job {job}, notice {notice_id}\")\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\"\n\n\ndef prepare_turn(*, profile_home, env=None):\n    if not _env_flag(env):\n        return [], \"\"\n    mailbox = mailbox_dir(profile_home)\n    inflight = mailbox / _INFLIGHT\n    _recover_inflight(mailbox, inflight)\n    if not mailbox.is_dir():\n        return [], \"\"\n    waiting = sorted(mailbox.glob(\"*.json\"), key=_order_key)\n    if not waiting:\n        return [], \"\"\n    # Parse every notice before staging any of them: a corrupt receipt aborts\n    # the turn with the mailbox untouched.\n    notices = [_load_notice(p) for p in waiting]\n    inflight.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    for p in waiting:\n        os.replace(p, inflight / p.name)\n    return notices, format_context(notices)\n\n\ndef consume_turn(*, profile_home, env=None):\n    if not _env_flag(env):\n        return 0\n    inflight = inflight_dir(profile_home)\n    if not inflight.is_dir():\n        return 0\n    removed = 0\n    for p in inflight.glob(\"*.json\"):\n        try:\n            p.unlink()\n            removed += 1\n        except FileNotFoundError:\n            pass\n    try:\n        inflight.rmdir()\n    except OSError:\n        pass\n    return removed\n```\n\nWait: `_load_notice` uses json.load; corrupt raises JSONDecodeError subclass of ValueError. Test expects (JSONDecodeError, OSError). Good.\n\nBut json.load on non-dict returns and we raise ValueError \u2014 not in expected tuple. Test D6 uses invalid text, so JSONDecodeError. Fine. Could change to json.JSONDecodeError for non-dict to be safe? Not needed.\n\nHmm: `_order_key` calls stat; if