r arbitrary. So sorting by filename gives FIFO. Alternatively sort by (mtime, name). Let's sort by filename primarily; if two files have same name impossible. But what if filename prefix is a creation timestamp in a different order? Let me also consider sorting by st_mtime_ns then name, which would be robust to any naming. But mtime granularity on Linux is ns and 0.01s gap is plenty. However recovery via os.replace preserves mtime? os.replace preserves mtime? Rename preserves inode and timestamps (mtime unchanged). Yes, renaming doesn't change mtime. So sorting by mtime is safe across recovery. But if two notices created within same mtime granularity, tie-break by name.\n\nHmm, but the st_mtime could be affected by... send_notice writes file then maybe fsync. Fine.\n\nWhich is more correct? The bar says \"drained in FIFO order\". The producer defines order. I think name sort is intended given zero-padded prefix. Let me sort by name, which also matches D6's name. But consider: if send_notice uses names like `<timestamp>-<uuid>` where timestamp is like `20261106T...` zero-padded, sorting by name works too.\n\nPotential tie: multiple notices created within the same timestamp resolution. Test sleep 0.01 ensures distinct if resolution is ms or finer; if the writer uses seconds, both could collide. But writer likely uses time.time_ns or monotonic_ns. Can't know. To be safer, sort by (mtime_ns, name)? If writer uses time.time_ns in the filename with a monotonic counter, name works; mtime also works. If writer uses seconds-resolution timestamp in name, mtime ns still distinguishes. So mtime sort is strictly more robust for FIFO. But is mtime reliable? Files created 0.01s apart will have distinct ns mtimes. Yes.\n\nBut hold on: what if send_notice writes to temp then renames? mtime of final file = write time. Fine.\n\nHowever, recovery: os.replace from inflight to mailbox preserves mtime. Then drain sorts by mtime \u2014 recovered notice is older, so it comes before newly qu