ists \u2014 yes since we raise before moving any. Good. But what if there are prior inflight files recovered then a corrupt mailbox file: recovered files remain in mailbox (not moved back to inflight). Then we raise. Next successful turn re-drains them, no loss. Good, fail-closed.\n\nHmm, but D2: after first prepare, inflight has 1 file, mailbox empty. Second prepare: recover moves it back to mailbox. Then files = [that]. parse. stage. OK.\n\nBut wait: recovery uses os.replace from inflight to mailbox. Then glob picks it. Good.\n\nEdge: `prepare_turn` on empty nonexistent home: recover sees inflight nonexistent, mailbox not dir, return. No dirs created. D3 passes.\n\nD1: after staging, `_mailbox_dir(...).glob(\"*.json\")` empty (files moved to inflight). inflight has 1 json. Good. Note inflight dir is inside mailbox dir; `mailbox.glob(\"*.json\")` does not recurse, so inflight files not counted. \n\nD5: other_home mailbox has one file; conductor prepare ignores. Conductor mailbox may not exist \u2192 return empty. Other file untouched. Good.\n\nD6: corrupt file only; no inflight. parse raises JSONDecodeError. File remains. Good.\n\nNow D2 detail: `context1.count(notice_id) == 1`. Our format includes notice_id once. Good. But if notice_id is a substring of itself multiple times? No. Unless the format includes job_id that contains notice_id? job id \"job-6-d2\" doesn't. Fine.\n\nAlso D4: order notices by FIFO. Using mtime. The two files created 0.01s apart. mtime_ns distinct likely. But if send_notice writes both via a monotonic clock and names, mtime also distinct. Good.\n\nHmm, but what if the filesystem mtime resolution is coarse (e.g., 1s)? On ext4, ns resolution. On some tmpfs, ns too. tempfile.mkdtemp in /tmp, likely tmpfs with ns. Fine. To be extra safe, tie-break by name; if names encode order, that saves us when mtime equal. But if names are random UUIDs and mtime equal, can't determine \u2014 unavoidable.\n\nActually, there's risk that mtime ordering differs